This document is the **long-term memory** for SkillPilot, including the skill-graph explorer.  
It captures the *concepts and design decisions* that are **not obvious from the code alone**, so humans and LLMs can extend the project consistently across different learning domains.

### Project licensing

The binding Product Owner decision of 21 September 2026 is **Apache-2.0 for
our software; CC-BY-4.0 for our knowledge-landscape content**. Follow the fixed
allocation and boundaries in `LICENSING.md`: code, technical schemas,
developer documentation and functional coach instructions are Apache-2.0;
own graphs, goal texts, mappings, tasks, cards, didactic media and Whitepapers
are CC-BY-4.0. Use function, not file extension, to classify material. Full
texts are `LICENSE` and `LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt`. No general dual licensing or
additional per-goal license approval is required for clearly own material.
Do not relicense third-party sources, linked provider content, dependencies,
marks or private learner data. Provenance labels such as `AI-generated,
SkillPilot-curated` are not licenses or proof of ownership. License grants,
source/third-party rights checks and quality approvals remain separate.
New exports carry applicable licenses and attribution; preserve prior grants,
immutable published archives and historical review records.

---

## 1. Purpose of this project

SkillPilot is a **general open-source learning platform** that models domains as skill graphs.
Every landscape should:

1. Represent learning goals as a **DAG of competencies** (cluster + atomic goals).
2. Enable **smooth learning paths** via the `requires` frontier: no goal is suggested unless prerequisites are mastered.
3. Provide a **transparent mastery/progress view** that can later drive grading or certification models.
4. Be usable both by **human teachers/learners** and **LLM-based agents** (task generation, coaching, analytics).

The project started from the Hessian KC 2024 upper-secondary landscapes; that source tree is retired (§10.5). The repository now ships the roots listed in `curricula/curriculum_manifest.json`:

- **Gymnasium (DE)**: one canonical landscape per subject under `curricula/DE/Gymnasium/canonical/` (20 subjects plus the overview root), viewed through Bundesland- and duration-specific composition views. Retained state sources live under `curricula/DE/Gymnasium/input/<STATE>/` for all sixteen Länder plus `KMK`. Maturity differs widely per subject; the generated `docs/qa-ci/status/curriculum-quality-status.json` is authoritative.
- **Hessian roots**: the manifest still lists `Gymnasiale Mittelstufe` and `Gymnasiale Oberstufe` (DE, HE, G9). Retired Hessen upper-secondary compatibility views are described only by frozen metadata under `curricula/DE/Gymnasium/archive/`.
- **Other school forms and programmes**: Bavarian school forms, university programmes (TU München, TU Darmstadt, Uni Mannheim, Uni Heidelberg) and MIT OpenCourseWare foundations.
- **Sprachen CEFR (für Deutschsprachige)**: Englisch und Französisch als A1–C2 CEFR-Landschaften under `curricula/EU/CEFR/`.

All conventions described below are general enough to support other curricula, subjects, or languages; the mathematics graph remains the reference implementation.

> Foreign languages (Englisch/Französisch) follow a TF × Skill × GER schema: Themenfelder aus dem KC + sprachliche Skills (reading, listening, speaking, writing, mediation, intercultural) mit `tags` wie `skill:*` und `cefr:B1/B2/C1` (E ~ B1, Q GK ~ B2, LK ~ C1).  
> German Ziele wurden KC-konform operationalisiert (prüfbare Beschreibungen, z. B. Analyse, Vergleich, Deutung, Urteilsbildung) und mit einfachen `skill:*`-Tags (lesen, schreiben, sprechen, medien, sprachreflexion) versehen.  
> Geschichte ist komplett angelegt (E + Q1–Q4), je Themenfeld GK-/LK-Ziele: Überblick, Quellenanalyse, Strukturen/Ursachen, Vergleich, historisches Urteil, Erinnerung/Deutung.

---

## 2. Mental model of the skill graph

### 2.1 Nodes = learning goals

Each node is a **learning goal** (“Lernziel”). There are two “flavours”:

- **Atomic goals**  
  Small, assessable skills (e.g. _“mittlere Änderungsrate berechnen und deuten”_).
- **Cluster goals**  
  Aggregations that group atomic goals (e.g. all goals in one Themenfeld, or all E1-Analysis basics).

Atomic goals are what the student actually “masters”.  
Cluster goals are for navigation, progress summaries, and intermediate hierarchy layers.

### 2.2 Edges = relationships between goals

We use exactly **two connection types**:

1. `requires` – **prerequisite**  
   - `A.requires = [B, C]` means: A should only be approached if B und C sitzen.  
   - Current compatibility model: existing prerequisites on clusters are still read through `contains` inheritance and atomic expansion for runtime/frontier purposes. This is transition semantics, not the canonical target state.
   - Binding target state (Product Owner, 8 November 2026): **all** authored `requires` have atomic source and prerequisite goals, $R\subseteq A\times A$. There is no permanent exception for universal cluster claims.
   - Product Owner decision of **12 November 2026** supersedes the earlier prerequisite-migration order and preservation obligation: acceptance depends on comprehensive deterministic validation of the current target state within the milestone examination. No comparison with a historical prerequisite closure, scope or frontier is required for `G17` or `GD-C17`.
   - Product Owner decision of **13 November 2026**: the grading domain of the Graph Definition `GD-*` register is the complete canonical Gymnasium catalog (21 roots). A rule is hard only when every obligation is fail-closed within that domain, applying milestone-bound obligations at their stated threshold. The complete policy pins 21 curricula and twelve route scopes (ten M6/M7 subjects, five of them M7). Landscapes outside Gymnasium alone do not justify `PARTIAL`; real runtime or subject-matter gaps remain explicit. Retained source inputs must remain byte-identical; their live consumers and any inventory exclusion need explicit validation. This programme neither repairs nor retires non-Gymnasium landscapes and does not promote the eleven M0 curricula. Every upgraded register row names its gate, covered count, and negative test. The immutable V9 proof and all learner-result contracts remain unchanged.
   - Before any `M1`–`M7` pass, check the complete declared canonical subject inventory, including necessary foreign prerequisites and inheriting ancestors, for resolved identities/references, acyclicity, atomic `requires` endpoints, consistent explicit types and transitive minimality. A violation or incomplete check caps the subject and its scopes at `M0`; an incomplete catalog aborts the status run. Current `CQR-001` coverage is the canonical Gymnasium catalog, not every curriculum in the repository.
   - Historical reduction proofs remain evidence for the review bindings that actually reuse them; they neither define the current validation inventory nor replace current-state checks. A missing or invalid proof disables that reuse and leaves strict current-input QA checks in force; it never grants stale reviews. Existing source fidelity, review freshness and learner-result contracts remain in force. Graph edits never automatically write learner results.
   - Product Owner decision of **12 November 2026 (frozen proof, registry contract V9)**: the reduction proof is frozen at its end state and review reuse is decided goal by goal. Contracts V1 to V8 bind complete documents and store no historical document, so their chain can be replayed only while the catalog still is its end state; any goal change, even a new exam, disabled the whole reuse. The chain was verified completely for the last time at the freeze. The active registry `curricula/DE/Gymnasium/provenance/prerequisite-reduction-proof-registry.json` (`frozen-goal-scoped-prerequisite-reduction-v9`) binds the complete V8 proof as its parent by raw digest and records, for each of the 1,361 goals whose reviews saw another value than the frozen goal, that reviewed value and the digest of the frozen goal. While a goal still has its frozen digest, its reviews are compared with the reviewed value. A changed or removed goal has no transfer and faces the strict current-input checks, so only that goal needs a new review. Every other catalog change (new goals, new documents, other goals, learning units) leaves all transfers untouched. Witness paths belong to the frozen proof and are not re-checked against later catalogs; ordinary reviews do not depend on edges of other goals either. The V9 registry and its parent are immutable evidence: never edit or rebind them. No process replays the history after the freeze; a later bulk normalization needs a new proof epoch. Record: `docs/dev/prerequisite-reduction-proof-freeze-2026-11-12.md`.
   - Intention: modelling **didactic dependencies** (prevents jumping into topics without foundation).

2. `contains` – **is composed of / includes**  
   - `A.contains = [B, C]` means: A is a **higher-level bundle** of B and C.  
   - Cluster progress and satisfaction are derived from the atomic descendants. Completing a cluster never writes mastery back to its children.

The overall structure is a **DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph)**:
- `requires` should never form cycles.
- `contains` also should not create cycles (think of it as a hierarchy of clusters).

### 2.3 Separate goals from structure and taxonomy

The long-term target model should distinguish four semantic concepts plus one learner-facing composition layer that are currently still partly mixed in some landscapes:

- **Goal layer**: actual assessable learning goals and fachliche clusters
- **Program layer**: structural units such as year, semester, module, phase, track, or exam
- **Placement layer**: links from goals into program units
- **Competency-axis layer**: taxonomy entries such as process competencies (`K1`-`K6`)
- **Composition-view layer**: explicit scope-specific learner-facing tree definitions that reference reviewed canonical subtrees

Interpretation rules:

- year/semester/module/phase nodes are **not** the durable semantic backbone of the skill graph
- broad capability families such as `K1`-`K6` are **not** program units
- only concrete, assessable process skills become actual goals
- current `phase` values should increasingly be treated as compatibility/view metadata rather than as the canonical semantic anchor
- user-facing initial narrowing by school form, stage, jurisdiction, duration model, course profile, and current year/phase should be treated as **entry scope** over program units, placements, and applicability, not as duplicated goal semantics
- catalog navigation (for example Schule / Hochschule / Sprachen) precedes the
  selected base curriculum and is not learner scope; the committed Personal
  Curriculum is backend-owned Level 2 state, edited through the authored flow
  in first-party WebGUI surfaces. The OpenAI V1 coach neither presents nor
  mutates that configuration
- Level 2 contains jurisdiction or canonical view, applicable duration model,
  explicit stage, selected subjects, and course profile per subject; an absent
  stage stays unresolved, and LK is a subject attribute that never implies
  Sekundarstufe II
- For the current Bavarian Mathematics rollout, `GK` and `LK` are **technical
  SkillPilot projection labels**, not the names of Bavarian courses. `GK`
  targets the complete compulsory four-hour Mathematics curriculum at elevated
  level; `LK` targets those goals **plus all five modules** of the separate
  Mathematics Vertiefungskurs. A teacher may use Level 3 focus to work on the
  three modules actually taught, but this does not remove the other two from
  the Level 2 target, total progress, or completion. The later elective-course
  and module-selection UX is tracked in GitHub issue #59. This interim mapping
  never relaxes goal quality, source fidelity, independent reviews, or strict
  Mathematics M7 gates; it does not assert that Bavaria offers Mathematics as
  a selectable Leistungsfach.
- For the Chemistry rollout, the Product Owner decided on **16 October 2026**
  to include all authored topics in the applicable target scope first and defer
  the preceding topic-selection flow, following the Bavarian Mathematics
  approach. This includes Schleswig-Holstein's optional medication theme in
  the interim GK/LK Chemistry target. Preserve its official optional status:
  inclusion in SkillPilot's broader target does not make it a universal official
  curriculum requirement or prove that a learner selected it. No persisted
  theme choice or new runtime selector is implied. Source fidelity, independent
  reviews, full target progress and strict Chemistry M7 gates remain required.
- current focus roots and the one active atomic goal are mutable Level 3 state;
  changing Level 2 revalidates them against the new learner-facing `target`
  projection without rewriting stable goal IDs or global Level 4 mastery
- Under the **10 November 2026 Product Owner decision**, the Personal Curriculum
  learning basis is $L=S\cup\{p\mid\exists t\in S:(t,p)\in R_{at}^+\}$,
  where $S$ is the union of canonical atomic seed targets from the selected
  reviewed subject scopes. First resolve valid seeds with their reviewed
  course/jurisdiction filters; then close on the unpruned canonical graph.
  Retain the additional necessary atoms even when those seed filters would
  exclude them; unrelated course or jurisdiction content is not included. Every necessary atom belongs to learning, progress and
  completion; an open prerequisite needs an actual acquisition target in the
  full Personal Curriculum. Deduplicate stable canonical IDs across subjects.
- $L$ is fixed by personal configuration and curriculum/projection revision,
  independently of mastery. Already mastered prerequisites stay in the progress
  denominator as satisfied and need no automatic repetition. Do not stop closure
  at a mastered intermediate. An assumed entry basis is not mastery evidence.
  Official curricular assignment and source applicability remain separate from
  inclusion as necessary preparation; no goal, edge, source claim or learner
  result is authored by this policy.
- all targets in this closed Personal Curriculum remain intended learning goals;
  Level 3 focus only narrows current work. Adding necessary targets does not
  widen focus automatically or change normal/strict prerequisite checks. Overall
  progress and completion still include necessary targets outside that focus.
- A Level 3 focus is the union of its selected roots and their `contains`
  descendants, restricted to Personal Curriculum targets. Normal mode checks
  only the effective prerequisite relation inside this focus, including
  scope-relative atomic satisfaction of prerequisite clusters. Requirements
  outside the focus, including `prerequisiteOnly` goals, do not block entry or
  become mastered. Explicit strict mode retains global prerequisite checks.
  This applies equally to a single root and to multiple exam/topic roots;
  invalid authored Level 2 references remain configuration errors.
- Level 3 focus widening follows the single learner-facing `contains` path
  toward the root. The backend publishes strict supersets that add at least one
  unmastered target, nearest suitable ancestor first. It proposes widening only
  after actual current-focus completion, never from an empty frontier alone,
  and persists the published option only after learner acceptance. With
  multiple focus roots, widening replaces only the covered branch and the
  option's complete ordered `goalIds` payload retains every independent root
- `requires` alone never writes prerequisite mastery. Under the 4 November
  2026 correction, independent application in valid local assessment contracts
  derives globally effective mastery of ordinary prerequisites. Required-work
  recommendations and ordinary prerequisite checks use that effective result;
  focus widening must not reopen a recognized goal. Voluntary repetition remains
  available. Transitive minimality is mandatory; a mastered intermediate goal
  alone must not hide an open local assessment's transitive demand.
- learner-facing default trees for resolved scopes should preferably be compiled from reviewed composition views early enough that validation can run before UI rendering
- composition views should reference canonical subtree roots and should not inline authored atomic goals
- composition-view references may explicitly use `projectionRole: "target"` or `projectionRole: "prerequisiteOnly"`; an omitted role means `target`
- authored subject-view roles are not inferred from phase, year or stage; the
  explicit personal learning-closure rule above additionally derives the global
  target basis from existing reviewed `requires`, without rewriting those roles
- Authored seed references and subject-local role assignments remain validated.
  At the Personal Curriculum compile boundary, each necessary atom receives a
  derived target role with a checked canonical prerequisite path and recorded
  provenance; this satisfies its role/exposure obligation without a manual view
  reference for each atom. The 10 November decision supersedes the 7 November
  blanket manual-role requirement at this boundary. Unknown references, cycles,
  missing canonical atoms or unexposed/unaccounted derived targets remain hard
  errors, regardless of mastery or focus. The learner runtime and first-party
  WebGUI implement this closed basis. Applicability validation and complete
  native/curriculum/remote acceptance remain separate current-state checks.
- Product Owner decision of 11 November 2026: these hard errors belong to
  validation, CQR and native acceptance, not to learner reads. When the
  canonical graph of a learning context is not strictly compilable, the runtime
  keeps the reviewed seed scope without the derived closure, claims no learning
  basis, offers no goal whose demand is broken, and logs the failure once per
  curriculum and error code. State, results and export stay readable.
  This covers the many non-canonical curricula whose root cluster contains and
  requires its own orientation goal; their data is not changed by this rule.
- `prerequisiteOnly` remains a subject-local support role. A required atom may
  keep that role in one view when the same canonical atom is a target elsewhere
  in the full Personal Curriculum; acquisition counts once. It must not remain
  outside global $L$ merely because every subject view labels it support. Only
  actual targets contribute to the respective learning/progress/completion set.
- when overlapping composition references assign different projection roles, specificity decides: a direct `goalEntry` overrides a role inherited from a `canonicalSubtree`, and the nearest/deepest matching `canonicalSubtree` overrides a broader subtree; only at equal specificity does `target` win
- within one resolved learner-facing scope, the default tree should show each goal at most once and under at most one visible parent
- Bundesland-specific canonical supplement branches under a broad shared root may use `extendedData.applicabilityMappingInheritance = "boundary"` on the supplement cluster. This prevents old broad ancestor-cluster source mappings from being treated as source evidence for the supplement's atomic descendants, while direct mappings/provenance inside the branch and child-union visibility upward still count.
- Practice or assessment terminal goals may use `extendedData.applicabilityFromRequires = true` when their visibility should be derived exactly from their prerequisite goals. This is for local autonomy/exam endpoints, not for ordinary content atoms, and the resulting `assessment-requires` evidence is applicability evidence only, not source-coverage evidence.
- The applicability compiler additionally supports an explicit
  `extendedData.assessmentApplicabilityBasis = { kind: "allOfGoalApplicability", goalRefs: [...] }`
  for those atomic endpoints. It evaluates the referenced goals freshly through
  the existing applicability rules, including cluster child unions. The basis
  does not add prerequisite edges, learning targets, source coverage or mastery.
  Current support requires a fully resolved local expression and rejects
  malformed, duplicate, cyclic, foreign or Memory-derived dependencies. This is a temporary
  compiler boundary, not a restriction on foreign prerequisites in the Personal
  Curriculum. The V8 proof contract introduced the existing Biology and
  Economics basis bindings; the frozen V9 registry carries their reviewed
  values per goal. Ordinary current
  description-review contexts still reject an unqualified basis; the
  current-state decision does not extend the review schema. Those evidence
  bindings remain intact; new graph acceptance follows the current-state
  decision above and does not require another prerequisite-migration
  preservation proof. The milestone producer uses the frozen proof only for
  review reuse (registry contract V9, §2.2). The complete-proof verifier stays
  as a tool with its fixture regressions. The replays of the real history ran
  for the last time at the freeze and are retired; the native gates on the
  current catalog, among them `APV-102`, live in
  `CanonicalRequiredLearningGateTest`.
- local authoring tools should mirror this split: one tool for canonical cluster authoring, one tool for composition-view authoring
- preferred design maxim: **as much semantics as necessary, as little ontology as possible**
- add semantic distinctions only when they solve a concrete authoring, migration, validation, projection, or runtime problem

Reference:

- `docs/concept/skill-graph/general-goal-system-and-migration.md`
- `docs/dev/skill-graph-minimal-schema-runtime-fallback-and-math-pilot.md`

### 2.4 Recursive learning units

**Documentation status, 19 November 2026.** The two Skill Graph compendia
describe a proposal for expert review of representative unit assessments.
The documentation revision is authorized. Adoption of the proposal as the
product contract and subject review of the Hessian E.1 example are pending.
The confirmed November contracts in §§2.3–2.4, 4 and 10.2 still govern
curriculum authoring, learner results, runtime work and QA. The Graph Definition
keeps the applicable recognition formulas in [§13.2](docs/concept/skill-graph/assessment-gated-completion.md#sec-transitive-demand)
and [§13.4](docs/concept/skill-graph/assessment-gated-completion.md#sec-global-effect),
and the applicable validity rules R1–R5 in [§13.3](docs/concept/skill-graph/assessment-gated-completion.md#sec-why-graph-not-enough).
“Binding target model” below refers to the confirmed November contracts,
including the 14–15 November ordering and stage-plan decisions; it does not
adopt the separate unit-assessment proposal of 19 November. The [discussion
paper](docs/concept/skill-graph/unit-assessment-discussion-2026-11-19.de.md)
sets out the changes that adoption would require.

**Guided unit entry, Product Owner decision of 22 November 2026.** Each
pedagogical unit with assessments of its own has one atomic orientation goal.
On entering the unit, show its place, an illustrated situation, a short topic
overview and its learning promise. Browsing changes no learner result. Guided
study starts with this goal. Content, exercises and flashcard practice follow
its personal completion or a valid, version-bound planning credit for that
orientation. A learner can speculate, ask a question or go straight to the
closing offer. The coach summarizes the outlook and records the binary result
only after the learner accepts that offer. On return, use existing evidence
and offer the next step. A green check records a personally completed opening.
An indirect credit has a gray check. A preparation waiver grants neither.

Apply the rule to the unit actually entered. Guided descent from a parent
introduces the parent and then the child. Direct entry into a child introduces
the child with a short reference to the parent. Structural groupings give
location without an extra atomic goal. A deliberate direct assessment attempt
can start before orientation. Leaving before submission returns to the guided
path with its outstanding opening. In the E.1 unit assignment, A–D suffice.
The first implementation covers E.1 and its assessed child in Hessen G8/G9.

**Learner-facing hierarchy, 21 November 2026.** The target for a given learner
and scope is one containment hierarchy shared by the Cockpit tree and the
graphical learning map. The same unit has the same children and an atomic
goal opens under the same parent in both views. Prerequisite arrows, source
references and links to practice material do not create an additional owner.
A presentation group containing only a single atomic learning goal must not
create a navigable learning unit around it. Open the goal directly and retain
its context, direct actions and evidence. Keep declared programme stages,
source scope and independent unit promises, assessments and ordering intact.
Verify parity against actual displayed parent paths in the same scope as the
tree and map converge. The learner tree places visible prerequisites and authored unit steps before their successors, including within nested units. Independent siblings retain a stable order.

**Learner-facing evidence marks for all subjects and scopes.** A green check
means the learner has their own successful evidence for this goal. The same check in gray
means that assessed work on another goal gives a
documented credit for this one. On 23 November 2026 the Product Owner removed
the minus sign. An atom with no individual evidence or reviewed credit keeps
its open or active mark, including when the current assignment can finish
without it. Tree, map, counts and accessible labels report this consistently.
Goal and unit tiles offer ordinary navigation without practice buttons.
Show a certified section's completion separately from how many individual
goals have their own evidence. Display choices never write mastery.

**Hessian E.1 pilot decision, 20 November 2026.** For an explicitly selected
unit-qualification assignment, passing the four designated versions A–D ends
the required work for that assignment. An individually open goal remains
available voluntarily or due under a separate explicit assignment. A valid historical part receipt
counts towards its part after a scope change. If it lacks the planning-release
binding at grading, the cockpit and coach explain why the two individual
goals remain open. A separate due assignment takes priority on a request to
continue. Once the unit assignment is complete and no such work is due, offer
a choice of individually open targets of the same unit for voluntary study.
The additional horizontal-throw examination remains available voluntarily and
has its own result. The local acceptance preview opts into the pilot. Its
separate runtime setting defaults to off. The Product Owner reserves the
pedagogical acceptance of E.1 and subsequent acceptance of the Hessian template.

**Section selections in atomic plans, 21 November 2026.** With the unit
assessment pilot enabled, a section selection contributes the goals required
by its applicable assessment policy. This applies when reading an existing
plan as well as creating a new one. An old E.1 section selection that captured
the voluntary horizontal-throw exam contributes 16 positions instead of 17
in the GK example. An explicit selection of that exam still counts, including
in older plans that stored it only once across overlapping blocks. Stored
selections, assessment receipts and individual mastery are preserved. With
the pilot disabled, plans use their stored goal lists.

The Product Owner confirmed **Lerneinheit** (English **learning unit**,
conceptual name `LearningUnit`) on **4 November 2026**. This replaces the former
curricular-level terminology. Personalization Levels 1–4, QA maturity and goal
`level` difficulty retain their meanings.

- A unit states what learners will be able to do and why it matters. A motivational
  introduction leads into goals and optional subunits. Calendar years, semesters
  and navigation headings are not automatically assessed learning units.
- Assign unit roles through authoritative placements or reviewed composition
  structure for a resolved scope/revision. Every acquisition target has one
  direct owner in that hierarchy. Ancestors aggregate child targets; general
  canonical multiple-parent containment is still permitted.
- Derive unit completion from all required child completions and its own
  goal-type results. No extra unit mastery is stored. A parent may add an
  integrative assessment; an aggregate needs no redundant extra examination.
- Local recognition applies only to directly owned ordinary targets under the
  reviewed assessment contract. Never turn a parent's descendant union into a
  second shortcut. Valid direct and implicit competence remains globally effective
  while a later or parent exam is open. Exams and cards always need their own
  explicit results; a parent pass never passes them implicitly.
- **Whole-unit order (B9, 14 November 2026).** Only an explicitly authored
  `after` states the intended full completion of one unit before another
  starts; it is order, never a new whole-unit prerequisite type. Atomic
  prerequisites demand only their competencies. The former undecided access
  profile is retired in the binding target model: `after` blocks no starts
  or direct assessment attempts and changes neither frontier nor mastery.
  Task availability, authentication, scope and session binding remain their
  own contracts. This does not claim removal of historical code paths;
  independent branches may proceed in parallel.
- **Composition child order (16 November 2026).** Source composition format
  `1.2` supports an optional `structure.childOrder.after` list of
  `{unit, after}` pairs naming direct structure children. It declares a partial
  planning order; an empty list declares no additional child order. Actual
  atomic demand still applies and must satisfy E3. Without this
  field, legacy sibling-order behavior remains. Checked stage-plan bindings
  retain priority. Filtering must preserve reachability through removed
  intermediate children and reduce the visible order transitively. Unknown,
  self, duplicate, cyclic or transitively redundant declarations fail
  authoring validation. This changes neither canonical `requires` nor learner
  scope, frontier, mastery or task access. Do not downgrade the new source
  format into an older export contract that cannot retain its order.
  Author a justified partial order, never an adjacency chain copied from the
  display list. Check E3 against actual cross-unit demand: empty declarations
  are not a shortcut for leaving required ordering unexplained. Repair an
  incoherent unit grouping before declaring whole-unit sequencing.
- **Explicit acquisition placement (16 November 2026).** In composition format
  `1.2`, a nested atomic `goalEntry` with `projectionRole: prerequisiteOnly`
  may declare `acquisitionPlacement: true`. This preserves its reviewed learning
  place as an internal presentation template. Only the backend's closed Personal
  Curriculum learning basis authorizes materialization, and an already visible
  target placement wins. Among multiple explicit templates, the goal's owning
  subject takes precedence over a foreign-subject fallback; wrapper depth
  across subjects is not curricular authority. Check the combined map for
  earlier demand and repair inappropriate authored timing. The flag creates
  no seeds, prerequisites or mastery.
  Unmarked support entries, global support declarations and broad support
  subtrees remain scope metadata; their location alone is never acquisition
  authority. Public views without Personal Curriculum metadata stay unchanged.
- Preserve effective atomic acyclicity and transitive minimality. When whole-unit
  sequencing is intended, additionally check the projected block graph at every
  relevant boundary: atomic acyclicity alone does not guarantee a unit order.
  Include inherited, indirect, cluster, hidden and cross-subject requirements.
- Optional workload claims need documented entry assumptions, supported effort
  estimates, reserve and overall/subject budgets. Count shared acquisition once;
  `weight` and raw goal counts are not time estimates. An assumed entry competency
  is not a learner mastery write.
- **Purpose (Product Owner, 11 November 2026).** A unit establishes solid,
  independent application of its promise before planned learning builds on it.
  Its assessments expose essential misconceptions; a gap leads to focused
  learning and a fresh equivalent assessment instead of a false completion. Unit
  boundaries follow meaningful conceptual transitions, not existing exams,
  target quotas, tidy headings or edge density; never add or remove edges to
  fit a cut. The ordinary grading contract applies: no hidden pass rule and no
  second stored outcome. This is a didactic requirement, not an access lock.
- **Unit validity rules (Product Owner, 11 November 2026).** Recognition (§4)
  is only sound if units hard-satisfy five rules, each with a fixed placement:
  - R1: every own ordinary content goal of a unit lies on a prerequisite path
    from at least one own assessment of that unit, directly or transitively; a
    path that leads only to a memory goal does not count. Validity condition of
    the unit definition, same class as acyclicity: hard from the first authored
    unit; a unit that violates it cannot be merged.
  - R2: for every own ordinary goal, at least one own assessment that requires
    it has reviewed evidence that its tasks and normal grading really test the
    goal. Milestone M5 (assessment approval and coverage metadata), with the
    current per-goal binding at M7; without it the curriculum does not reach
    the milestone.
  - R3: an assessment claims coverage only for goals it requires directly or
    transitively (coverage ⊆ Reach⁺ over the atomic prerequisite relation), so
    the assessment that actually tests a goal is among those the recognition
    rule waits for. Same placement as R1.
  - R4: every acquisition target has exactly one directly owning unit. Same
    placement as R1.
  - R5: a later re-cut of units or later edges never grant additional
    recognition to existing results; the contract is versioned. Runtime
    contract and precondition before implicit recognition is enabled in
    production.
  Implicit recognition exists only inside units whose contract satisfies R1–R4
  with reviewed coverage. Otherwise the unit's goals stay open, and the
  violation keeps the curriculum from reaching the milestone. A goal that no
  assessment covers therefore stays open *and* is a rule violation; it is never
  recognized for free ([Graph Definition §13.3](docs/concept/skill-graph/assessment-gated-completion.md#sec-why-graph-not-enough);
  `GD-U05`, `GD-U06`, `GD-U15`,
  `GD-A07`, `GD-A08`, `GD-A10`, `GD-A12`, `GD-A18`).
- **Resumption (Product Owner, 12 November 2026).** The learning-unit work is
  resumed; the working plan is
  `docs/dev/mathematics-physics-learning-units-plan.md` (Mathematics first, then
  Physics). The catalog of units is derived in-house, block by block, from the
  canonical graph and the design sheet; an external proposal is at most a
  recorded origin, never a contract. The whole-unit access question left open
  at that date was retired by B9 on 14 November 2026; only declared `after`
  orders whole-unit completion, without need or access locks.
- **Unit contract (Product Owner, 12 November 2026).** Learning units are part
  of the landscape and live in the existing placement layer; the Product Owner
  rejected a separate catalog file beside the landscape as a bolt-on and asked
  for a stable long-term contract, with a new schema version wherever one is
  needed. A unit is a `programUnits` entry of kind `learningUnit` (`id`,
  `label`, `labelEn`, `promise`, `promiseEn`, `revision`, optional
  `parentUnitId` naming the unit that requires it as a child). It owns atomic
  goals of the same landscape through `goalPlacements` of relation `own`,
  which carry exactly `goalId`, `unitId` and `relation` (no `context`). Reviewed coverage (R2) is the unit's
  `coverageReviews`, one entry per own assessment, hash-bound to that
  assessment's task, solution and rubric; goal objects carry nothing of the
  unit contract, so a review never changes a goal's bytes. The
  optional landscape field `learningUnitCoverage` is `partial` or `complete`.
  The declaration is closed: whole-unit prerequisites, access locks and waiver
  lists cannot be expressed. Curriculum-package contract 1.2
  (`contracts/curriculum-package/v1.2`) carries the units; builds under 1.0 or
  1.1 project them out completely, and published files of earlier versions
  stay unchanged. The completion profiles and scopes of contract 1.1 implement
  the superseded waiver semantics and remain in operation only until the
  runtime switches.
- **Unit validator (12 November 2026).** `npm --prefix app run
  validate:learning-units` enforces R1, R3 and R4 plus the declaration rules
  fail-closed for every learning unit in the canonical landscapes and runs in
  the required curriculum CI; only `learningUnitCoverage: "complete"` requires
  an owner for every atomic goal. Missing R2 evidence is reported, not
  rejected; it is not yet a milestone gate. R5 and implicit recognition are
  not implemented: a learning unit has no runtime effect, and every goal is
  still completed by its own explicit result. Never add an edge, weaken a goal
  description or cut a unit to turn the validator green; a finding is a
  subject-review task.
- **Bindings ignore the learning-unit layer (12 November 2026).** Evidence
  about goals, edges and assessments binds the landscape with its
  learning-unit layer projected out: without `programUnits` of kind
  `learningUnit`, without `goalPlacements` of relation `own` or towards a
  learning unit, and without `learningUnitCoverage`. It is the projection the
  package build applies for contracts 1.0 and 1.1; for a landscape without
  units it is the identity. Contracts V1 to V8 of the prerequisite-reduction
  proof and the input pin of the Mathematics duration layout fingerprint that
  projection; a registry record keeps its full digest. Since the freeze of
  12 November 2026 the active V9 registry binds single goals (§2.2), so
  neither a unit edit nor an unrelated goal change stales review reuse; the
  duration-layout pin still goes stale on any goal change in Mathematics.
  Carriers that predate learning units follow the same rule: a 1.0 or
  1.1 build and the legacy subject export (with its MEM/FWU roundtrip) ship
  the landscape without the layer; only contract 1.2 carries units. This was
  the recommendation presented to the Product Owner on 12 November 2026 after
  the first placement attempt broke the proof chain; the Product Owner asked
  to proceed. Never rebind a proof by hand and never touch a floor to place a
  unit. Record: `docs/dev/canonical-math-learning-unit-pilot-binding-review-2026-11-12.md`.
- **Redistribution ledger (Product Owner, 12 November 2026).** The committed,
  CI-checked package-redistribution ledger is always the one of the contract
  under which packages are released: today 1.0. The 1.2 ledger binds the 1.2
  model, because a redistribution review must see everything that ships; it
  is generated per build and is committed, taking over the CI check, when the
  runtime switches to contract 1.2. Do not commit a ledger that no CI step
  checks.
- **First placed unit (12 November 2026), first reviewed coverage
  (13 November 2026).** Canonical Mathematics contains the pilot unit
  `math-dot-product-projection` (one ordinary goal, one assessment). Its first
  assessment did not satisfy R2
  (`curricula/DE/Gymnasium/quality/learning-units/math-dot-product-projection-2026-11-12.md`).
  Since 13 November the unit owns the assessment
  `be48054d-48f7-54c0-8248-d0a462ad6dc9`, written for it, and carries the
  first coverage review: two independent machine reviewers, the second without
  knowledge of the first, found R2 satisfied for the bound text
  (`curricula/DE/Gymnasium/quality/learning-units/math-dot-product-projection-2026-11-13.md`).
  The earlier assessment keeps its ID outside the unit; its passes are not
  upgraded. A coverage review is machine content review bound to the task and
  rubric revisions: any change to task, solution, grading note or rubric needs
  a new review. The earlier completion profile `math-dot-product-projection`
  still names the earlier assessment and stays unchanged until the runtime
  stage retires the profile path: production runs in automatic mode, which
  skips the completion-profile and completion-scope directories, so the
  profile only serves the historical profile-path tests and the 1.1 package
  profile.
- **Second unit and the assessment tool (13 November 2026).** Canonical
  Mathematics also contains `math-natural-numbers` (five grade 5 goals, two
  assessments written for it, `49507f95-d079-5113-8aac-1a08299473a3` and
  `de7e0bb2-262a-50e7-8398-f91ab1b365ca`), with reviewed coverage for all five
  goals
  (`curricula/DE/Gymnasium/quality/learning-units/math-natural-numbers-2026-11-13.md`).
  Unit assessments are authored in a source file under
  `curricula/DE/Gymnasium/assessments/` and placed by
  `app/scripts/applyLearningUnitAssessments.mjs`, which derives the assessment
  goal, its cluster membership, the semantic-kind decision, the unit and its
  coverage reviews; `npm --prefix app run check:learning-unit-assessments`
  fails in required CI when catalog and sources differ. Do not edit a unit
  assessment in the canonical file by hand. The pilot stays a unit of its own
  (decided 13 November): its goal is a learning target in few configurations,
  and in a shared unit its assessment would hold back the recognition of the
  three core dot-product goals everywhere. The assessment model has one task
  per assessment ID; fresh task variants for repeat attempts need a contract
  extension and backend selection and belong to the runtime stage.
- **First Physics unit (13 November 2026) and experimental goals (Product
  Owner, 14 November 2026).** Canonical Physics contains
  `physics-constant-acceleration` (two goals of the introductory phase, one
  assessment written for it, `c5807a37-129e-5df6-8989-482bc7f5683e`) with
  reviewed coverage for both goals
  (`curricula/DE/Gymnasium/quality/learning-units/physics-constant-acceleration-2026-11-13.md`).
  The goal "Freier Fall experimentell untersuchen" is outside the unit: two
  independent reviewers found R2 not satisfied for an assessment that
  evaluates given measurement data, and disagreed on whether own measurement
  data or planning plus evaluation would satisfy it. Experimental goals get
  no special treatment (Product Owner, 14 November 2026; this replaces the
  decision of the same day that they are never recognized). An experimental
  goal is a content goal like any other: it joins a unit once an own
  assessment of that unit requires it and a coverage review confirms that the
  assessment tests it, and it is then recognized like the unit's other goals.
  Until then it stays outside the unit. What an assessment must demand to
  test a unit assessment remains subject to the review of its exact task.
  On 23 November 2026 the Product Owner confirmed that experimental subject
  goals are ordinary required targets in the applicable curriculum. Author a
  feasible home investigation or supply an equivalent route for the named
  skill, for example a documented dataset in the chat. An external simulation
  can support an appropriate modelling question. Learners identify the data
  source and do the interpretation themselves. Record personal setup or data
  acquisition only when the learner carried it out. The assessment credit for
  each goal depends on the actual reviewed performance. The contract needs no marker
  and no `ownExplicit` relation.
- **Knowledge context of a unit (13 November 2026).** Nothing is written by
  hand about the subject matter of a unit: its knowledge context is the union
  of the contexts of its own goals and assessments (§2.5). Tasks, memory
  cards, goal images and coach examples of a unit stay inside it; whether an
  assessment does becomes part of its R2 review, not a new validity rule.
- **Unit structure (Product Owner decision, 14 November 2026; not
  implemented).** `docs/concept/skill-graph/learning-unit-wiring-concept-2026-11-14.md`
  is the binding statement (decisions B1 to B16). Three concepts build on each
  other: a cluster structures goals; a unit is a cluster that is learned in
  one piece (begun, completed, and only then what lies after it) and thereby
  takes part in the order; a unit with assessments additionally proves the
  independent application of its own content goals. Being a unit is declared
  in the unit entry of the placement layer, which points to the cluster; own
  goals and sub-units follow from `contains`. Not every cluster is a unit,
  and subjects are not. A unit has assessments when its entry names coverage
  reviews for own assessments; these assessments lie in its cluster (they
  move there; view rule 5 rules out a second parent cluster) and cover each
  of its own content goals (R1 to R5 unchanged). A unit without assessments
  only orders its goals and may hold content goals directly; in the end every
  content goal lies in a unit with assessments. Only content goals need a
  unit. The children of a unit are its direct sub-units and its own goals;
  there are no uniform levels. Subject units live in the canonical landscape
  and hold in every Land; stages, school years and phases are plan units of a
  Land across the selected subjects, in a fixed order. Between two units that
  are children of the same unit the relation is `after` (the intended order:
  the other unit is completed before this one begins); between atomic goals
  it is `requires`, which is a special `after` that additionally states the
  need. An `after` holds for all goals of its two units, as an order and
  never as a need, and no cluster carries `requires`. Rules E1 to E4 keep the
  two consistent, each looking at the children of one unit (E1: units form a
  tree; E2: an order of all goals exists that keeps every need and every
  `after` with each unit in one piece; E3: the `requires` between goals of
  different units are a subset of the `after` inherited to the leaves, that
  is, where a need separates two child units of the smallest unit that
  contains both goals, the `after` between them is declared; E4: no redundant
  declaration among the children of a unit). An `after` never changes the
  frontier and locks nothing. In the learning mode "unit by unit" it decides
  which reachable goal comes next: first from a child that is due (everything
  it must come after is done), among those from a unit already begun. That a
  unit builds on the independent application of another is said only by its
  `after` and followed by the learning mode: no `requires` points to an
  assessment goal, and there is no unit lock, so the open access profile of
  Graph Definition §11.5 is dropped. The 170 existing edges from ordinary
  goals to assessments contradict this and go to the subject review. Every Land
  carries a subject unit without sub-units whole in one plan unit. The
  learner map shows units and atomic goals only: for display, the learner's
  graph is cleaned of the clusters that are not units, `contains` is kept
  across the removed clusters, and subjects act as filters. All clusters
  appear in the goal tree. Units are introduced top-down: plan units first
  (only the plans of the Länder change), subject units below them, their
  assessments last. There is no timeline and no stage-specific rule. Never
  author `requires` on a cluster of a canonical landscape: today's readers
  inherit it to every atomic goal and `CQR-001` rejects it; inheritance stays
  the reading of transition data outside the canonical Gymnasium catalog.
  Until the follow-up work in section 10 of the document is done, the tools
  enforce the unit contract of 12 November. The applicable rules in Graph
  Definition §§11–13 distinguish the confirmed binding target model from that
  implemented compatibility contract; target wording does not assert completed
  migration. The separately marked 19 November proposal has not replaced either
  contract.
  A first, surface-only step is implemented: the learner map
  (`app/src/utils/learnerNavigation/learnerUnitMapProjection.ts` and
  `learnerUnitMapPreview.ts`) renders the learner's graph as units and atomic
  goals for Mathematics and Physics only, under a visible construction-site
  notice; other subjects appear in the goal tree. Tree siblings follow the shared
  projected prerequisite and study order wherever the map shows those siblings. Units are not declared in the curriculum yet, so this preview
  takes the sections of the Land views as stand-ins, always at the deepest
  top-down step (the step control was removed on 17 November 2026), orders
  sibling sections as the view lists them, and merges the two
  stages across the subjects through a provisional table of view node ids
  (`PROVISIONAL_STAGE_PLAN_UNITS`), to be removed once plan units are
  declared. `buildLearnerMapProjection` is kept but no longer rendered.


- **Stage authority and normative precedence (15 November 2026).** The F7
  bilingual Graph Definition source states the binding target model and
  labels the previous 1.2 ownership representation as compatibility. One
  shared plan per Land and duration model governs all selected subjects;
  course profiles may select contributions but cannot contradict the stage
  of a stable goal. Its full root has only Sekundarstufe I and II, exactly
  II `after` I; orientation, practice and assessment goals belong inside
  these stages. A single-stage view is a derived excerpt; the nationwide
  canonical structure is no Land plan. `Geführt in` means source-evidenced
  curricular assignment; `steht in` means learning placement. A canonical
  phase may justify an explicitly provisional default, never official
  coverage. A support reference's authored position asserts no stage.
  Necessary atoms outside the carried seed targets receive one derived
  placement at their earliest actual need in the closed personal scope,
  independently of mastery. Already carried late targets are not silently
  moved; their backflows remain findings until explicitly repaired.
  E1 to E4 apply per declaration layer and to derived preparation. R4 is
  E1; R1 to R5 govern units with assessments. Assessment-free units only
  order and recognize nothing. `GD-U14`'s formerly open access profile is
  retired in the target model, without claiming historical code removal.
  The stage-plan foundation under `contracts/curriculum-package/v1.3/` is
  not a published package version. Zero real Land plans are activated;
  shared production-reader migration remains open. Subject-unit migration,
  B3/B13 acceptance, packages, recognition and learning-mode runtime require
  F8 to F13 and their own evidence. Existing atomic G17 across the complete
  21-root catalog, immutable V9 evidence, quality floors, focus rules,
  learner results and recognition semantics remain unchanged.

This is concept documentation plus a static authoring contract and validator,
not an implemented runtime or access policy. See `docs/concept/skill-graph/learning-units.md`,
`docs/concept/skill-graph/learning-unit-progression.md`,
`docs/concept/skill-graph/learning-unit-design-and-examples.md` (design criteria
and worked candidates) and Graph Definition §11 (learning units), §12 (optional
