## 4. Mastery model

The **4 November 2026 Product Owner decision** supersedes the 31 October
preparation-discharge model. Transitive minimality remains mandatory. Direct
ordinary mastery establishes understanding; implicit mastery establishes the
competence through independent application in its relevant passed assessments.
Both mean completed globally: effective mastery satisfies later prerequisites
and counts toward total progress. Later consumers, focus changes and a different
Personal Curriculum do not withdraw a valid recognition.

Store only the existing binary result per atomic goal: `mastery[goalId] ∈ {0,1}`.
Here `0` means no explicit completion; a goal may nevertheless be effectively
mastered through derived recognition. Let M be explicit mastery and I the
recognition derived from fixed authoritative learning-unit contracts; use M_eff = M ∪ I
for competence, ordinary prerequisites and total progress. This is not a second
stored outcome, partial mastery, a higher numeric tier or a separate pass.
Assessment and memory goals themselves are never implicit: use their normal
binary grading or verified-recall results.

Recognition considers every transitive consumer within the authoritative
directly owning learning unit. Traverse full effective prerequisite paths through mastered
intermediate goals. In Q → B → A, ordinary B alone mastered does not discharge
A while the local exam Q is open. Once all relevant local assessment/card
obligations are met, A and B can be recognized and remain completed for later
units. A child unit can finish while a later or parent exam is open. A temporary
focus is not a learning unit. Uncovered ordinary endpoints are authoring
faults, never zero-work recognition; a failed structural calculation gives no
new implicit credit. Required card targets remain explicit independently.

**Confirmation of 11 November 2026 (Product Owner).** This recognition rule
stays: implicit mastery is derived per valid local contract of the owning
learning unit, and an own ordinary goal is recognized once it is no longer
required by any open own assessment or memory goal of that unit (`N_U`, `I_U`
in [Graph Definition §13.2](docs/concept/skill-graph/assessment-gated-completion.md#sec-transitive-demand)).
Example: assessments Q1 and Q2 belong to the same
unit and both require A; when Q1 is passed, A stays open until Q2 is passed as
well. The alternative recorded on the archived branch of PR #83 as a
"6 November 2026 target decision" (immediate recognition from the qualified
coverage list `C(q)` of a single passed assessment, including proof by a parent
or foreign unit) was reviewed and is **not adopted**. Do not port its formulas,
its rewrite of `docs/concept/skill-graph/latex/check-assessment-model.py` or its
diagrams. The rule is only sound inside units that satisfy the validity rules
R1–R5 of §2.4.

The normal tasks and rubrics MUST require independent application of every
competence recognized by the relevant assessment set. A missing essential
competence must not be compensated by unrelated points while it is recognized.
Independent review tests whether passing without a claimed competence is
possible. A requires path supplies structure, not proof of task quality.
No extra runtime qualifying evidence, manually curated waiver list, second
per-part result or mastery backfill is permitted. Unreviewed legacy tasks do
not automatically satisfy this stronger authoring contract.

Keep recognition semantics stable for each assessment identity and retain the
authoritative authorship context needed to reconstruct it outside today's
selected plan. New graph edges or a broader new unit must not grant additional
competencies to old exam results (rule R5: the contract is versioned, and this
runtime contract is a precondition before implicit recognition is enabled in
production). Equivalent task forms preserve that contract;
a different recognition claim requires a distinct assessment identity or an
explicitly reviewed migration. Do not invent a learner qualification registry.

See `docs/concept/skill-graph/assessment-gated-completion.md` and the decision
record `docs/concept/skill-graph/completion-semantics-clarification-2026-11-04.md`.
This is the corrected concept, not a claim that current main implements it.
Implementation was replanned on 12 November 2026 after the concept
reconciliation (`docs/dev/mathematics-physics-learning-units-plan.md`, §2.4);
old PR work is reused selectively. Do not continue the old PR's
runtime/curriculum rollout.

Cluster progress aggregates effective mastery of unique atomic descendants;
no cluster or unit completion writes mastery to its children.

For goals authoritatively classified as `semanticKind: "orientation"`, this
numeric field is only a compatibility completion marker: `1` means that the
learner received the motivation/orientation, either explicitly wished to
continue or actively engaged in a tailored motivational follow-up, and then
accepted a separate coach offer to close after a positive summary. Merely
repeating or selecting one of the possibilities offered by the coach starts
that follow-up; it does not complete the orientation. The coach must connect
the selected interest to concrete things the learner can understand, explore,
shape, or do, and invite one low-pressure personal reaction, choice, or
question. This does **not** certify subject-matter mastery. Orientation must
show concrete possibilities and honest positive perspectives of the following
material; it must not test prior knowledge, terminology, calculations,
details, correctness, transfer, recall, or exam performance. Do not expose a
graded 0/50/100 mastery control for these nodes.

Goal types determine how this one outcome is reached. Orientation follows the
interaction contract above. SRS/memorization goals (`srs-deck:*` /
`memorization`) derive their goal outcome from card-level review data under the
memory workflow; a coach must not manually assign it. Card scheduling and
review data describe the learning process, not additional goal-result states.

**Implementation status (30 October 2026):** new general mastery writes enforce
`0` or `1`, and ordinary atomic goal controls offer binary choices. Historical
numeric rows remain readable through the shared legacy threshold `>= 0.9`;
this does not introduce partial outcomes or round existing database values.
The technical completion pilot, protected card synchronization and import
boundaries are documented in `docs/dev/assessment-gated-completion-pilot.md`.
Goal-type workflow details remain in
`docs/concept/runtime-workflows/learning-workflow.md`.

The intended use is:

1. **Learner, teacher or coach** records whether an ordinary atomic goal is mastered under its completion contract.
2. The backend computes aggregate progress from the binary atomic outcomes.
3. A grading rule (not yet implemented) maps these to familiar school grades (1–6).

When agents generate feedback or suggestions, they should:

- Use the authoritative mastered/not-mastered result; do not invent degrees of mastery.
- Suggest unmastered goals whose effective prerequisites are satisfied under
  the selected normal or strict scope mode.

For coach-led learning, finishing a task and mastering a goal are distinct.
The coach makes its evidence decision privately when sufficient learner work
is available. For an ordinary content goal this can happen during the chat;
for an `examData` assessment goal it happens after grading the complete
submission. A solved task alone does not imply mastery, and a learner's
agreement is not evidence. Genuine multi-step transfer within one task can
suffice; count the evidence, not the number of task labels. Once every aspect
is sufficiently shown, stop assessing; there is no extra-task quota. When the
goal is mastered, commit the success
immediately, before claiming it was saved or announcing a successor. Do not
condition this write on a separate learner agreement to close. If the write
fails, do not claim that the goal was saved. Guide ordinary learning toward
mastery with targeted checks of any missing aspect. After a confirmed save,
say that the goal is mastered and saved, offer the backend-selected next topic
if available, and wait for the learner's answer. A learner may instead remain
for questions or optional unassessed practice without reopening saved mastery.
If evidence is missing, never offer mastery or a mastered next topic as a
learner choice. An accepted, authorized offer cannot be reversed by reflection
on unchanged work; report an actual session, state or write failure as such.
A request for the next topic after declining another task never accepts that
task. If the goal remains open, use only authorized redirect options and never
infer mastery from that request. An unsuccessful exam attempt
produces feedback but no learner-state write: SkillPilot does not distinguish
"not passed" from "not attempted", and the learner may retry the same exam
without a limit. After grading, disclose the score, result, tasks and solution
and discuss them on request. This can reduce the evidential value of a later
retry of the same exam; it is a known current limitation without a special
countermeasure. A verdict on the submitted work stays fixed unless the learner
provides new substantive work or an actual grading error is corrected; a
plain "weiter" does not trigger reassessment. After feedback, invite questions
or continuation and wait for the learner's reply before showing the next task
or goal. Apply this with or without autopilot. Answer questions about the
current content and respect a pause. Begin new content and render its image
only after recognizable agreement to continue, such as “Alles klar, weiter”.
At the actual end, offer a fitting close without assuming a next task. Small
explanations and hints during an unfinished task do not need separate
confirmation. Orientation and Verified Recall retain their goal-type workflows
for reaching the same single binary goal outcome.

